{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"},{"text":"Good — the `## Bot version follows` marker is still there; I'll assume that whole tail gets deleted once we've reconciled. Responses to your four:\n\n**1. Pushes.** You're right, and I overstated it. A push carries *what* and *where* as args, so distinct pushes get distinct keys — you won't cross-wire two different pushes into one no-op. The only residual is a *non-idempotent* effect (append-to-log, send-email, create-resource) re-run with identical args after the world moved: cache hit → skipped. But `git push` is idempotent (remote already has the commit ⇒ real no-op anyway), so caching matches reality. And the escape hatch is free: a tool that genuinely needs to re-fire adds a nonce/timestamp arg. So no first-class \"effectful\" flag needed. Dropping it — agreed.\n\n**3.** Agreed, cut it.\n\n**2.** Looks fine as an injection-at-start note.\n\n**4 — this is the real one, and I think you've found the actual reason for declaration.** It's *not* the authority reason we dismissed; it's cache-key hygiene. Let me state it sharply:\n\nYour last Solution bullet (\"visible secret names in the key\") is what makes the key reflect grant state — it's what gives you both rotation-doesn't-bust (value not in key) *and* coherence when a grant is added/revoked (name-set in key changes ⇒ key changes). But computing that set means the server must, at every dispatch, **superset-match the arg tree against the entire `.caos-secrets` store** to discover which secrets apply. The key then depends on the whole out-of-tree store.\n\nDeclaration fixes exactly that:\n\n- The tool names its candidate secrets **in the tree** (in its image/deps), so \"which secrets are relevant here\" is content-addressed and bounded — the server checks *only* the declared names against grants, never enumerates the whole store.\n- What still enters the key is the **granted subset of the declared names** — bounded, tree-anchored, and it's the part you *want* in the key (revocation coherence). Value stays out (rotation-safe).\n\nSo declaration and names-in-key aren't rivals: **declaration bounds and tree-anchors the set; you still fold the granted-subset-of-declared into the key.** Net: the key becomes ~a function of the tree plus a bounded grant lookup, instead of a function of the entire mutable secret store. That's the \"something in the image to bust the cache\" you were reaching for.\n\nHonest footnote: as *security* declaration still adds nothing (grant is the authority). Its whole justification is this cache-key one. And the cleanest semantics for declared-but-not-granted is **hard error** (fail fast, uncached), so you never cache a degraded result.\n\nSo I'd bring the declaration section back — reframed from \"audit anchor / both must agree\" to \"bounds the grant lookup and makes secret-relevance content-addressed for the cache key.\"\n\n**Syntax.** Since a reader is a *partial arg tree* (may pin args, not just a path), reuse the `.caos-expr` single-line grammar for it. And keep the opaque value out of any line-based format. So — directory, one dir per secret, two files:\n\n```\n.caos-secrets/github/value      # raw bytes, verbatim — the secret (multiline/binary safe)\n.caos-secrets/github/readers    # one arg-tree expr per line; '#' comments, blanks ignored\n```\n\n`readers` e.g.:\n```\nstd/github-push\ntools/deploy -- --repo=github.com/me/proj\n```\nEach line is eval-path'd to a canonical arg tree; superset-match is exact.\n\nCompact single-file alternative, echoing caos's `--`:\n```\n# .caos-secrets/github\nstd/github-push\ntools/deploy -- --repo=...\n--\nghp_realtokenbytes…\n```\nreaders above `--`, raw value below. Cute, but the two-file form is more robust for binary/multiline values and has no `--`-in-value ambiguity — I'd go two-file. (And leave room to let `value` be an indirection later — `env:GH_TOKEN`, a backend ref — rather than literal bytes, without changing `readers`.)\n\nWant me to reconcile the doc: delete the bot tail, and add a short reframed declaration section + a syntax section in your bullet style?","type":"text"}],"results":[{"content":[{"text":"# Secrets: identity-is-capability — design note\n\n**Status:** proposed. Design discussion only; not built. Builds on\n`.caos-expr` (eval-path, deep-deps) and map-then (server-mediated worker\nstarts).\n\n## Problem\n\nSome tools need secrets: the github-push tool needs an auth token, and there\nwill be many like it. But:\n- we don't want secrets in content-addressed stores, where they might leak\n- we don't want secrets in keys, because we don't want to invalidate (most) keys if a secret is rotated\n- we don't want secrets in one worker/arg tree to be able to be read from it by another worker\n\n## Solution\n\n- Secrets live in a git-ignored .caos-secrets directory\n- Each secret file contains the secret's value and a list of partial arg trees\n- A partial arg tree is described as a path to tree in git, which is evaluated with `caos eval-path`\n- A secret is visible to a worker if the worker's arg tree is a superset of one of the arg trees of the secret \n- If a secret is visible to a worker, it is injected into a worker in `/secret/<name>`\n- We attempt to scrub secret values from the logs of workers\n- We attempt to check files that are added to git with `caos put` for secret values. Any new file (hash not in git) that contains the value of a secret that is visible to this worker is rejected\n- The names of all secrets visible to a worker are included in the worker's cache key, but not the values. This means that a worker's result must not depend on the secret's value. For example, a worker can fail with an invalid secret, but it should not return a list of things, filtered to what is visible to this secret's account\n\n## Bot version follows\n\n## Declaration: secrets as deps\n\nA tool declares the secrets it uses in its deep-deps file, alongside ordinary\ndeps, one line each:\n\n```\nsecret/github\n```\n\nThis is **intent, not authority**. Its jobs: visible in review (\"this tool\nuses github\"), tells the server which secrets to inject and scrub for this\nrun, and it is the audit anchor. Declaration alone never resolves a secret —\notherwise capability is self-granting (anyone writes `secret/github`).","type":"text"}],"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J3DfJHbxCphhcapPAgdocF","type":"tool_result"}],"v":1}